.TH LST 8 2026-08-23 Lustre "Lustre Configuration Utilities"
.SH NAME
lst \- run the Lustre LNET self-test
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY lst
.SY lst
.B --list-commands
.SY lst
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENT "] ..."
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
LNET self-test helps site administrators confirm that Lustre
Networking (LNET) has been properly installed and configured.
The self-test also confirms that LNET and the network software
and hardware underlying it are performing according to expectations.
.LP
Every LNET self-test runs in the context of a session.  A node can be
associated with only one session at a time to ensure the session has
exclusive use of the nodes on which it is running. A session is created,
controlled and monitored from a single node; this is referred to as the
self-test console.
.LP
Any node may act as the self-test console.  Nodes are named and allocated
to a self-test session in groups.  This allows all nodes in a group to
be referenced by a single name.
.LP
Test configurations are built by describing and running test batches.
A test batch is simply a named collection of tests, with each test
composed of a number of individual point-to-point tests running in parallel.
These individual point-to-point tests are instantiated according to the
test type, source group, target group and distribution specified when the
test is added to the test batch.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --list-commands ", " list-commands
Output a list of the commands supported by the
.B lst
utility and exit.
.TP
.BR --help ", " help
Print a short usage summary.
.B "lst help"
.I COMMAND
prints the usage string of a single command.
.TP
.BR --version ", " version
Output the build version of the utility itself.
.TP
.BR --ignore_errors ", " ignore_errors
Ignore errors during script processing.  The flag is only consulted for
commands read from standard input by a single
.B lst
process, as in
.BR "lst < script" ,
so it belongs on the first line of that script rather than on the command
line.  It has no effect on a shell script that runs one
.B lst
per line, such as the one below.
.SH COMMANDS
Given a command,
.B lst
runs that single command against the current session and exits.  Invoked with
no arguments at all it reads commands from standard input instead, presenting
an interactive prompt when standard input is a terminal.  Run
.B lst --list-commands
for the full list of commands and
.B lst help
.I COMMAND
for the full usage string of one of them.  The synopses below show the
principal options only; the most commonly used commands are:
.TP
.BI "new_session [--timeout " SECONDS "] [--force] [" NAME ]
Create a new session.  A node may belong to only one session at a time;
.B --force
takes the nodes away from whatever session they are already in, running or
not, and
.B --timeout
sets how long the session survives without an
.B lst
command being run against it (default 300 seconds).
.I NAME
defaults to
.IR user @ host .
.TP
.B end_session
Stop all running batches and destroy the session, releasing every node.
.TP
.B show_session
Show the name, key and owning node of the current session.
.TP
.BI "add_group " NAME " " NIDS ...
Create a named group from a list of NIDs (for example
.BR 192.168.1.[1-253/2]@o2ib ).
Groups let a set of nodes be referenced by a single name.
.TP
.BI "list_group [--active] [--busy] [--down] [--unknown] [--all] [" NAME "] ..."
List the defined groups, or the members and status of the named groups.
.TP
.BI "del_group " NAME
Remove a group.
.TP
.BI "update_group {--refresh | --clean " STATE " | --remove " NIDS "} " NAME
Update an existing group.
.B --refresh
re-queries the state of its nodes,
.BI --clean " STATE"
drops the nodes that are in one state
.RB ( active ", " busy ", " down ", " unknown " or " invalid ),
and
.BI --remove " NIDS"
removes the listed nodes.  Exactly one of the three operations must be given.
There is no operation to add nodes to an existing group.
.TP
.BI "add_batch " NAME
Create an empty, named test batch.  A batch is a collection of tests that
are started, stopped and reported on together.
.TP
.BI "add_test [--batch " NAME "] --from " GROUP " --to " GROUP " \
[--concurrency " N "] [--distribute " SRC : DST "] [--loop " N "] " TEST " ..."
Add a test to a batch.  See
.B TEST PARAMETERS
below.
.TP
.BI "run [" NAME ]
Start running the tests in a batch (default
.BR batch ).
.TP
.BI "stop [--force] [" NAME ]
Stop a running batch (default
.BR batch ).
.TP
.BI "list_batch [" NAME "]"
List batches, or the tests and per-node state of one batch.
.TP
.BI "query [" NAME ]
Query the run state of the tests in a batch (default
.BR batch ).
.TP
.BI "stat [--rpc] [--mbs] [--delay " SECONDS "] [--count " N "] " NAME " ..."
Print live LNet send and receive rates and bandwidth for the named groups,
or for a list of NIDs, every
.B --delay
seconds (default 5) until interrupted or until
.B --count
samples have been printed.
.B --mbs
reports bandwidth in MB/s instead of MiB/s.  Only the LNet counters are
implemented, so
.B --rpc
prints nothing; error counters come from
.B show_error
below.
.TP
.BI "show_error " NAME " ..."
Show the accumulated error counters for the named groups, or for a list of
NIDs.
.TP
.BI "ping {--session | --group " NAME " | --batch " NAME " | --nodes " NIDS "}"
Probe the selected nodes and report whether they are reachable and which
session they belong to.  The nodes are always named by one of the selector
options above;
.B ping
takes no positional NIDs, and it probes the nodes of the current session
rather than performing a low-level LNET ping.
.SH TEST PARAMETERS
The
.B add_test
command takes a set of options that select the nodes and shape of the test,
followed by the test type and its type-specific parameters.
.SS add_test options
.TP
.BI "--batch " NAME
Batch to add the test to (defaults to the batch named
.BR batch ).
.TP
.BI "--from " GROUP
Source group: the nodes that initiate the test (the clients).
.TP
.BI "--to " GROUP
Target group: the nodes that service the test (the servers).
.TP
.BI "--concurrency " N
Number of concurrent RPCs each source node keeps in flight to each of its
target nodes (default 1).  A source node paired with several targets by
.B --distribute
therefore has that many times
.I N
RPCs outstanding.
.TP
.BI "--distribute " SRC : DST
Ratio that maps source nodes to target nodes, controlling the point-to-point
pairing between the two groups (default 1:1).
.TP
.BI "--loop " N
Number of iterations to run; a negative value (the default) loops until the
batch is stopped.
.SS Test type: ping
.B ping
takes no further parameters and drives small, payload-free RPCs between the
source and target groups.
.B lst stat
reports the resulting RPC rate; the individual round-trip times are only
written to the kernel debug log under
.BR D_NET .
.SS Test type: brw
.B brw
(bulk read/write) moves bulk payloads to measure throughput and, optionally, to
verify data integrity.  Its parameters follow the word
.B brw
as bare keywords such as
.B read
or as
.IR keyword = value
tokens:
.TP
.BR read | write " (or " r | w )
Direction of the bulk transfer.  With
.B read
the target group (server) sends and the source group (client) receives; with
.B write
the client sends and the server receives.  Default is
.BR read .
.TP
.BI "size=" BYTES
Size of each bulk transfer; the short form
.B s=
is also accepted.  A
.B K
or
.B M
suffix multiplies by 1024 or 1024*1024.  The maximum is one LNET MTU
(1 MiB) and the default is 4096.  The size must be a multiple of 8 bytes;
this is not checked, and an unaligned one asserts on the source node while it
fills the bulk, before any request is sent.
.TP
.BI "off=" BYTES
Offset within the client-side bulk buffer, as a multiple of 8 bytes
(default 0).  The server side always uses offset 0; the request carries no
offset field.  A non-zero offset is only safe when it fits in the
unused tail of the last page of the transfer, since the bulk descriptor is
sized from
.B size=
alone: with a
.B size=
that is a whole number of pages any non-zero offset overruns it.
.TP
.BR check=simple | full | discard " (or " c=... )
Data-integrity checking mode for the bulk payload.  If no
.B check=
parameter is given the payload is neither filled nor verified and only
throughput is measured; there is no
.B check=none
value to spell that case explicitly, and giving one is an error.
.RS
.TP
.B simple
Fill and verify a magic pattern in the first and last 8 bytes of each page.
Catches gross corruption cheaply.
.TP
.B full
Fill and verify the magic pattern across every 8 bytes of the payload.  Most
thorough, but the byte-by-byte fill and check cost CPU and lower the achievable
bandwidth.
.TP
.B discard
Peak-throughput measurement mode.  The node that
.I receives
the bulk data drops the incoming payload as it arrives \(em socklnd reads the
socket with
.B MSG_TRUNC
instead of copying the bytes into the destination pages \(em which removes the
receive-side memory-copy overhead that otherwise caps large-message bandwidth.
.IP
Because the payload is never delivered, this mode performs
.I no
data verification: it is effectively an unchecked test with the receive copy
skipped as well, so use it only to measure raw bandwidth, never to validate
data integrity.
.IP
The discard takes effect only on the side that receives the bulk, i.e. the
.I sink:
for
.B brw read
that is the client (the
.B --from
group) and for
.B brw write
it is the server (the
.B --to
group).  It is currently honored only by the socket LND, so the receiving
side must communicate over a socklnd network
.RB ( @tcp ", " @tcp1 ", ...);"
on other LNDs (for example o2iblnd or kfilnd) the parameter is
accepted but silently has no effect and the test behaves like an ordinary
unchecked bulk test.
.RE
.TP
.BR skip_rx_copy | skip-rx-copy
Select the discard mode above.  These are bulk parameters in their own right
and not
.B check=
values, so
.B check=skip_rx_copy
is rejected.  Combining either of them with a
.B check=
mode other than
.B discard
is an error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LST_SESSION
The session key.  It must be set (normally to the shell PID,
.BR "export LST_SESSION=$$" )
before any command is issued; commands that need a session refuse to run
without it.
.TP
.B LST_FEATURES
Hexadecimal mask of the optional session features to enable, 0x1 being
variable bulk length.  Defaults to all supported features; lower it when
older test nodes reject the session.  A session without variable bulk length
falls back to the original bulk request, which carries no offset and only a
page count, so
.B off=
is ignored and
.B size=
is rounded up to a whole number of pages.
.SH MODULES
To run LNET self-test, load these modules: libcfs, lnet, lnet_selftest
and any one of the klnds (i.e, ksocklnd, ko2iblnd...).
To load all necessary modules, run modprobe lnet_selftest, which
recursively loads the modules that lnet_selftest depends on.
.LP
There are two types of nodes for LNET self-test: the console node and
test nodes. Both node types require all previously-specified modules
to be loaded. (The userspace test node does not require these modules.)
.LP
A console user can invite a test node to join the test session by running
lst add_group \fINID\fR.
.LP
.SH UTILITIES
.B lst
is the user interface for the self-test console (run on console node).
It provides a list of commands to control the entire test system,
such as create session, create test groups, etc.
.LP
.SH EXAMPLE SCRIPT
Below is a sample LNET self-test script which simulates the traffic
pattern of a set of Lustre servers on a TCP network, accessed by Lustre
clients on an IB network (connected via LNET routers), with half the
clients reading and half the clients writing.
.LP
.nf
#!/usr/bin/bash
export LST_SESSION=$$
lst new_session read/write
lst add_group servers 192.168.10.[8,10,12-16]@tcp
lst add_group readers 192.168.1.[1-253/2]@o2ib
lst add_group writers 192.168.1.[2-254/2]@o2ib
lst add_batch bulk_rw
lst add_test --batch bulk_rw --from readers --to servers \e
    brw read check=simple size=1M
lst add_test --batch bulk_rw --from writers --to servers \e
    brw write check=full size=4K
# Measure peak write bandwidth with no receive-side copy or checking.
# The sink here is the "servers" group, which is on @tcp, so the socklnd
# discard actually takes effect (it would be a no-op on the @o2ib readers).
# Use a separate batch so this test does not contend with the two above.
lst add_batch bulk_discard
lst add_test --batch bulk_discard --from writers --to servers \e
    brw write check=discard size=1M
# run the checked tests and display server stats for 30 seconds
lst run bulk_rw
lst stat servers & sleep 30; kill $!
lst stop bulk_rw
# then measure the discard bandwidth on its own
lst run bulk_discard
lst stat servers & sleep 30; kill $!
lst stop bulk_discard
# tear down
lst end_session
.fi
